  "id": "fig-228a",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Inspect a generic proton-synchrotron ring schematic: four curved magnet sectors, straight regions, upper accelerating device, injection and extraction. Geometric regularization is a layout choice, not a measured historical facility plan.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/00b1/lemmadeck/ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-228a.png",
      "sha256": "30e2beac18aa789e30dc4d70feef9e5db91d608b4027c5d0c80efaa5d9c01195"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0267#2",
        "text": "回旋加速器是前面学过的一种粒子加速器。\n它利用这样一个性质：在低速近似下，带电粒子在磁场中做圆周运动的周期与圆周半径 $R$（或粒子的速度 $v$）无关。\n这个周期由粒子的质量、电荷量和磁场的磁感应强度决定。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0267#3",
        "text": "周期不随速度改变的结论只适用于 $v\\ll c$ 的低速近似。\n当 $v$ 很大时，相对论效应使固定磁场中的回旋周期增大，但粒子的静止质量并不改变。\n因此，固定磁场、固定加速频率的普通回旋加速器不能继续保持同步，把粒子加速到任意高的能量。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0267#4",
        "text": "原书把本课介绍的高能加速器称为**同步回旋加速器**；按现代分类，这种固定环形轨道、随加速增强磁场的质子加速器属于**质子同步加速器**。\n它与回旋加速器一样，让带电粒子（通常是质子）反复通过具有强交变电场的加速区，每次通过都获得能量。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0267#5,p0267#8",
        "text": "这种加速器具有很大的环形结构。\n粒子在真空室内运动，真空室位于电磁铁系统中。\n粒子的轨迹由短直线段和弧线段组成（图 228a）。\n\n一些直线段上设有电极，电极之间的电场用来加速粒子。\n另一些直线段上装有把粒子注入加速器、以及把达到相对论速度的粒子束引出的设备。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0267#7",
        "text": "图 228a"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0267#9,p0268#1",
        "text": "质子每次通过加速区都会获得能量，动量也随之增加。\n相对论动量等于洛伦兹因子、静止质量与速度的乘积，其中静止质量保持不变。\n在粒子电荷量和轨道曲率半径固定时，弯转磁场必须随动量增大而增强。\n\n加速区的射频交变电场也必须与粒子运动同步，使质子到达加速间隙时电场沿着它的运动方向。\n环形轨道的周长固定，所以射频频率应始终保持为粒子每秒绕行次数的固定整数倍。\n质子速度增大时，绕行频率和射频频率随之提高，而速度接近光速时频率逐渐接近上限。\n\n磁场强度和射频频率的协调变化需要按相对论动力学确定。\n这正是本课所述加速器必须使用相对论动力学来描述的原因。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0268#3",
        "text": "图 $228$b"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0268#4",
        "text": "图 $228$b 展示的是杜勃纳市联合核研究所的一百亿电子伏特①质子同步加速器的概貌。\n这里的联合核研究所位于杜勃纳市，文字及图示保留的是原书选用的历史实例。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0268#6",
        "text": "① **电子伏特**（$\\boldsymbol{eV}$）是国际单位制外的能量单位，在核物理学中广泛使用。\n其精确换算关系为 $1$ 电子伏特 $=1.602176634\\times10^{-19}$ 焦耳。\n一个电子经过 $1$ 伏特的加速电位差时，获得 $1$ 电子伏特的能量。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/zh/p0268#5",
        "text": "原书从当时苏联的视角，把谢尔普霍夫市附近的同步加速器称为“我国当前最大的加速器”。\n这是原书的历史表述，不指今天的中国或今天的世界排名；该装置的磁铁总质量记为 $20000$ 吨。\n\n质子每绕行一周，路程约为 $1.5$ 千米。\n这一周内累计通过的加速电位差为 $350000$ 伏，因此质子获得 $350000$ 电子伏特的能量。\n原书记载，在整个加速循环中，质子获得的能量大于 $700$ 亿电子伏特。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0267#2",
        "text": "The cyclotron is a particle accelerator introduced earlier.\nIt uses the fact that, in the low-speed approximation, the period of a charged particle's circular motion in a magnetic field does not depend on the orbit radius $R$ (or the particle's speed $v$).\nThe period is determined by the particle's mass, its electric charge, and the magnetic flux density."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0267#3",
        "text": "The conclusion that the period is independent of speed applies only in the low-speed approximation $v\\ll c$.\nAt large $v$, relativistic effects increase the revolution period in a fixed magnetic field, but the particle's rest mass does not change.\nAn ordinary cyclotron with a fixed magnetic field and a fixed accelerating frequency therefore loses synchronism and cannot accelerate particles to arbitrarily high energies."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0267#4",
        "text": "The source calls the high-energy accelerator in this lesson a **synchrocyclotron**; in modern terminology, the described proton machine, with a fixed ring orbit and an increasing magnetic field during acceleration, is a **proton synchrotron**.\nLike a cyclotron, it sends charged particles, usually protons, repeatedly through a region with a strong alternating electric field, so they gain energy on each passage."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0267#5,p0267#8",
        "text": "This accelerator has a large ring-shaped structure.\nParticles travel inside a vacuum chamber located within a system of electromagnets.\nTheir path consists of short straight sections and curved sections (Figure 228a).\n\nSome straight sections contain electrodes whose electric field accelerates the particles.\nOther straight sections contain equipment for injecting particles into the accelerator and extracting the beam after it reaches relativistic speeds."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0267#7",
        "text": "Figure 228a"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0267#9,p0268#1",
        "text": "A proton gains energy and momentum each time it passes through the accelerating region.\nRelativistic momentum is the Lorentz factor multiplied by the rest mass and the speed, with the rest mass remaining unchanged.\nFor a fixed particle charge and a fixed bending radius, the bending magnetic field must increase as the momentum increases.\n\nThe alternating radio-frequency electric field must also stay synchronized with the particle motion, so that it points along a proton's motion when the proton reaches the accelerating gap.\nBecause the orbit circumference is fixed, the radio frequency must remain a fixed integer multiple of the number of revolutions per second.\nAs protons speed up, their revolution frequency and the radio frequency increase, approaching a limit as the speed approaches the speed of light.\n\nThe coordinated changes in magnetic field strength and radio frequency must be determined using relativistic dynamics.\nThis is why the accelerator described in this lesson requires a relativistic description."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0268#3",
        "text": "Figure $228$b"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0268#4",
        "text": "Figure $228$b shows the ten-billion-electron-volt① proton synchrotron at the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research in Dubna.\nThe institute is located in Dubna, and this text and figure preserve the historical example selected by the source."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0268#6",
        "text": "① The **electron-volt** ($\\boldsymbol{eV}$) is a non-SI unit of energy widely used in nuclear physics.\nIts exact conversion is $1$ electron-volt $=1.602176634\\times10^{-19}$ joule.\nAn electron gains $1$ electron-volt of energy when it crosses an accelerating potential difference of $1$ volt."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n100/en/p0268#5",
        "text": "Writing from the perspective of the Soviet Union at the time, the source calls the synchrotron near Serpukhov \"our country's largest accelerator at present\".\nThis is a historical statement, not a claim about present-day China or a current world ranking; the source gives the total magnet mass as $20000$ metric tonnes.\n\nA proton travels about $1.5$ kilometres in each revolution.\nDuring that revolution, it crosses a total accelerating potential difference of $350000$ volts and therefore gains $350000$ electron-volts of energy.\nThe source states that over the entire acceleration cycle, the proton gains more than $700$ times a hundred million electron-volts."
      }
    ],
